WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:05.370
So we've been tracking the rise of corona
virus cases in the United States and we started

00:00:05.370 --> 00:00:08.420
seeing the numbers spike on June 19th.

00:00:08.420 --> 00:00:15.150
There was the idea in late April, early May,
that that was the peak when we had 30000 new

00:00:15.150 --> 00:00:17.380
cases a day in April, May.

00:00:17.380 --> 00:00:18.580
We were hoping, I guess.

00:00:18.580 --> 00:00:22.880
I mean, I certainly was hoping that we were
past the peak, at least the wave one peak.

00:00:22.880 --> 00:00:28.789
And it looked like maybe we were until July,
June 19th, when we started seeing cases climb

00:00:28.789 --> 00:00:36.260
and climb and climb up to as many as 60 thousand
new cases a day a few days ago, centered around

00:00:36.260 --> 00:00:43.170
California and a bunch of red states, including
Arizona, Florida and Texas at the top of that

00:00:43.170 --> 00:00:44.170
list.

00:00:44.170 --> 00:00:49.130
And what we observed for the last 20 or so
days was that even though the cases were spiking,

00:00:49.130 --> 00:00:50.690
the deaths were staying pretty low.

00:00:50.690 --> 00:00:56.350
And you can see if you're looking at the chart
of deaths, they've been trending down.

00:00:56.350 --> 00:01:00.699
And all along, there were questions about
how is this possible, what's going on.

00:01:00.699 --> 00:01:04.930
Now, the most obvious explanation, maybe we
would call it the simplest explanation.

00:01:04.930 --> 00:01:09.890
We could say the explanation that requires
the fewest assumptions is that there is simply

00:01:09.890 --> 00:01:12.510
a lag between cases and deaths.

00:01:12.510 --> 00:01:14.650
You don't die the day you're diagnosed.

00:01:14.650 --> 00:01:19.890
So when you see cases spike, you would expect
that it would take some time before those

00:01:19.890 --> 00:01:22.880
increased case numbers turn into deaths.

00:01:22.880 --> 00:01:25.420
There were other explanations that we considered
as well.

00:01:25.420 --> 00:01:29.040
Is it possible that the virus has mutated
to be less deadly?

00:01:29.040 --> 00:01:31.490
It's possible we didn't have any evidence
for it.

00:01:31.490 --> 00:01:35.240
Is it possible that doctors are getting better
at treating corona virus patients, which is

00:01:35.240 --> 00:01:37.040
decreasing the death rate?

00:01:37.040 --> 00:01:38.130
Certainly possible.

00:01:38.130 --> 00:01:41.890
Could it be that more testing means healthier
people are getting tested?

00:01:41.890 --> 00:01:47.290
So even though you have more cases, the share
of cases that are serious could be declining.

00:01:47.290 --> 00:01:49.820
And that's why the deaths continue to trend
down.

00:01:49.820 --> 00:01:55.310
Or is it younger people getting corona virus
who are less likely to get really sick?

00:01:55.310 --> 00:01:59.210
All of these are reasonable possibilities
to wonder about.

00:01:59.210 --> 00:02:05.410
But if the explanation is the first one, which
is there is a lag between cases and deaths,

00:02:05.410 --> 00:02:12.000
we would expect that somewhere in the range
of 18 to 21 days after the cases spike, deaths

00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:16.950
would start to spike 18 to 21 days from the
start of the case.

00:02:16.950 --> 00:02:18.200
Spike is this week.

00:02:18.200 --> 00:02:19.200
It's yesterday.

00:02:19.200 --> 00:02:20.200
It's today.

00:02:20.200 --> 00:02:21.200
It's tomorrow.

00:02:21.200 --> 00:02:29.090
And sadly, tragically, yesterday, Arizona
and Texas both hit single day death records

00:02:29.090 --> 00:02:32.130
dating all the way back to the start of the
pandemic.

00:02:32.130 --> 00:02:40.420
Yesterday, we had again nearly 60000 new cases
of corona virus reported and deaths in Arizona

00:02:40.420 --> 00:02:41.740
and Texas.

00:02:41.740 --> 00:02:46.070
And by the way, also in California, California
is not exempt from this, but it's been mostly

00:02:46.070 --> 00:02:50.040
the red state are saying that it's Arizona
and Texas that weren't going to see the death

00:02:50.040 --> 00:02:51.290
spike.

00:02:51.290 --> 00:02:54.640
They are spiking Arizona with 117 deaths.

00:02:54.640 --> 00:02:58.120
Yesterday, Texas with 84 deaths yesterday.

00:02:58.120 --> 00:02:59.650
Those are sad.

00:02:59.650 --> 00:03:01.450
These are not records we want to be setting.

00:03:01.450 --> 00:03:05.150
These are tragic new high marks for a corona
virus.

00:03:05.150 --> 00:03:06.360
Deaths in a single day.

00:03:06.360 --> 00:03:11.120
Now, I don't want to be alarmist and I hope
that I'm wrong and I believe that I've been

00:03:11.120 --> 00:03:16.460
relatively sober in my analysis of Corona
virus from the start.

00:03:16.460 --> 00:03:23.290
The simplest explanation is that the deaths
are spiking because the cases spiked 20 days

00:03:23.290 --> 00:03:24.569
ago.

00:03:24.569 --> 00:03:29.350
And if that's the case in another 10 days,
the death numbers are going to look really,

00:03:29.350 --> 00:03:30.350
really bad.

00:03:30.350 --> 00:03:34.590
And what I mean by that is cases started to
spike on June 19.

00:03:34.590 --> 00:03:39.360
So the small spike in deaths we're seeing
now would be related to the start of the spike

00:03:39.360 --> 00:03:43.310
in cases on June 19th, around July 1st.

00:03:43.310 --> 00:03:47.630
The new case numbers were dramatically higher
than on June 19th.

00:03:47.630 --> 00:03:54.790
And if that tracks as a leading indicator
to eventual deaths in another 10 or 15 days,

00:03:54.790 --> 00:03:59.670
we are going to have unconscionable numbers
coming out of some of these states.

00:03:59.670 --> 00:04:03.550
It's horrible when we're right about terrible
things.

00:04:03.550 --> 00:04:05.270
And this is really, really terrible.

00:04:05.270 --> 00:04:09.870
Now, if we want to find something to look
at that looks OK.

00:04:09.870 --> 00:04:12.910
There are still some states doing quite well
right now.

00:04:12.910 --> 00:04:18.140
The states with the lowest reproduction rate
of the virus are Connecticut, Washington,

00:04:18.140 --> 00:04:20.090
D.C., which is not a state.

00:04:20.090 --> 00:04:25.880
Maine, New Hampshire, Massachusetts, Rhode
Island, Maryland and Utah, although the reproduction

00:04:25.880 --> 00:04:27.160
rate there, what's called the.

00:04:27.160 --> 00:04:28.160
Are not.

00:04:28.160 --> 00:04:29.160
The R zero.

00:04:29.160 --> 00:04:30.460
It's it's starting to creep up.

00:04:30.460 --> 00:04:35.280
And what I believe is going on is nothing
that requires any kind of particular insight.

00:04:35.280 --> 00:04:41.550
The states that are doing well can only hold
off cases for so long if other states are

00:04:41.550 --> 00:04:47.430
a disaster, because travel between states
is constitutionally it's a constitutional

00:04:47.430 --> 00:04:51.660
right in the United States, ingress and egress
from in two states.

00:04:51.660 --> 00:04:56.850
And when you look at the county level map
of the United States and you see, you know,

00:04:56.850 --> 00:05:01.430
the red and orange counties, the ones that
are in trouble, they're right next to counties

00:05:01.430 --> 00:05:02.900
that are doing far better.

00:05:02.900 --> 00:05:09.340
And so I believe that, sadly, unless there
are some interstate travel restrictions put

00:05:09.340 --> 00:05:13.870
in place, which I'm neither recommending nor
do I believe would necessarily be legal, I'm

00:05:13.870 --> 00:05:16.640
merely making them sort of mathematical statement.

00:05:16.640 --> 00:05:20.040
You're going to see some of the states that
are doing well start to do less.

00:05:20.040 --> 00:05:26.630
Well, if cases continue to skyrocket in places
like Florida, Arizona, California, Texas and

00:05:26.630 --> 00:05:29.320
many others, I can't even give you the full
list.

00:05:29.320 --> 00:05:34.650
Now, I do think also that there are some governors,
including our governor here.

00:05:34.650 --> 00:05:40.520
I believe Charlie Baker here in Massachusetts
has widely done a very good job.

00:05:40.520 --> 00:05:45.620
And what I'm worried about is they're now
doing the indoor dining in Massachusetts.

00:05:45.620 --> 00:05:49.270
I got a message from my gym that it's opening
next Monday.

00:05:49.270 --> 00:05:50.870
I think that this is a mistake.

00:05:50.870 --> 00:05:55.090
We've done so much work here for the last
four months to get down to only having a couple

00:05:55.090 --> 00:05:57.180
hundred new cases a day without a spike.

00:05:57.180 --> 00:06:01.330
It's like every day about 200 cases, 200 cases.

00:06:01.330 --> 00:06:05.930
It seems to me, given that it's summer, given
that people can do outdoor dining.

00:06:05.930 --> 00:06:09.750
Given that people can do outdoor workouts,
I understand the business side of it.

00:06:09.750 --> 00:06:14.180
It seems too risky to me to do indoor dining
or to open gyms, movie theaters and some of

00:06:14.180 --> 00:06:15.460
the other things being discussed.

00:06:15.460 --> 00:06:16.650
I won't be partaking.

00:06:16.650 --> 00:06:18.490
I've been doing the outdoor dining.

00:06:18.490 --> 00:06:19.740
It feels safe.

00:06:19.740 --> 00:06:21.180
It's more than enough.

00:06:21.180 --> 00:06:22.180
It's warm out.

00:06:22.180 --> 00:06:27.479
I think that that's a risk for the states
that are doing well to do right now.

00:06:27.479 --> 00:06:29.370
And I hope that I'm wrong, but the numbers
are truly, truly scary.

